{"sounds":{"intro":"This is Classical Nahuatl, the language of the Aztec (Mexica) empire, as written down by Spanish and native scholars in the 1500s and 1600s. Millions of people still speak the modern Nahuatl languages, which differ from it. The spelling here is the common scholars' one. Pronunciation is reconstructed, and vowel length and the glottal stop are not shown.","rows":[["tl","a single sound, like \"t\" and \"l\" together, as in the end of \"atlas\"","atl, tlalli"],["tz","like \"ts\" in \"cats\"","tzontli"],["x","like \"sh\" in \"ship\"","xochitl"],["c / qu","like \"k\"; \"qu\" is used before e and i","calli, quemmanian"],["hu / uh","like \"w\" in \"water\"","huei, cuahuitl"],["cu","like \"kw\" in \"quick\"","cualli"],["ll","a long \"l\" sound","calli"],["h","a small catch or breath in the throat, like the pause in \"uh-oh\"","ahmo, nehuatl"],["z / s","like \"s\" in \"sun\"","tlazohcamati"]],"notes":["The stress is almost always on the second last syllable: ca-LLI, to-NA-lli.","Most nouns end in -tl, -tli or -li when they stand alone. The plural often ends in -h or -meh.","Words are built from pieces. The verb in \"nicnequi\" (I want it) is ni- (I) + c- (it) + nequi (want)."]},"numbers":[{"id":"1-to-10","title":"1 to 10","items":[["one","ce","seh"],["two","ome","OH-meh"],["three","yei","YEH-ee"],["four","nahui","NAH-wee"],["five","macuilli","mah-KWEE-lee"],["six","chicuace","chee-KWAH-seh"],["seven","chicome","chee-KOH-meh"],["eight","chicuei","chee-KWEH-ee"],["nine","chiucnahui","cheeuk-NAH-wee"],["ten","matlactli","mah-TLAHK-tlee"]]},{"id":"bigger-numbers","title":"Bigger numbers","items":[["eleven","matlactli once","mah-TLAHK-tlee OHN-seh"],["twenty","cempohualli","sem-poh-WAH-lee"],["four hundred","centzontli","sen-TSOHN-tlee"],["eight thousand","cenxiquipilli","sen-shee-kee-PEE-lee"]]}],"greetings":[{"id":"greetings","title":"Greetings","items":[["hello","niltze","NEEL-tseh"],["good day","cualli tonalli","KWAH-lee toh-NAH-lee"],["good night","cualli